# Both files are regenerated and then diffed against what is committed -- by CI,
# and by anyone running the same checks locally. Without a fixed line ending the
# generators write LF onto a CRLF checkout and the diff fires on every Windows
# machine, reporting drift that is not there.
schema/src/generated.ts          text eol=lf
worker/worker-configuration.d.ts text eol=lf

# Both installers are served to a shell, so their line endings are part of what
# is published rather than a checkout detail. A CRLF install.sh fails on the
# first line with an error naming no cause, and this repo's primary environment
# is the one whose checkout would introduce them.
site/public/install.sh  text eol=lf
site/public/install.ps1 text eol=lf

# The wordmark's source and the two files scripts/generate-wordmark.ts writes
# from it. Most of this repo is checked out CRLF and none of it minds; this art
# does, because it is measured in columns and a carriage return is a byte on the
# end of every row that nobody can see.
#
# The document is pinned since #60, where it stopped being prose that named
# itself as the source and became the file two others are generated from. The
# generator drops carriage returns on the way in as well, which is not the same
# thing said twice: the pin is what stops a checkout carrying them, and dropping
# them is what stops a checkout that predates the pin writing them into two
# files pinned against them.
docs/design/DESIGN.md text eol=lf
site/lib/content.ts text eol=lf
cli/src/wordmark.ts text eol=lf

# The two subset webfonts, and the licence that has to travel with them.
#
# Git would infer the fonts are binary and would probably be right, but every
# other line in this file says what it means rather than leaving it to a
# heuristic, and these are the first committed binaries in the repo -- the
# compiled binaries under cli/dist are ignored, not tracked. A .woff2 that
# something decided was text and normalised the line endings of is a font that
# no longer parses, and the error it produces names none of that.
#
# The licence is pinned to LF for the reason the wordmark's sources are: it is
# fetched byte-for-byte from upstream and checksummed against a digest written
# into scripts/build-fonts.ts, and a checkout that rewrote its endings would
# fail that check on a machine rather than in a diff.
site/public/fonts/*.woff2 binary
site/public/fonts/OFL.txt text eol=lf

# The face #83's harness serves in place of the real one, for the same reason as
# the two above. It is cut from the same source and carries printable ASCII and
# no Block Element at all, which is the subsetting mistake the whole arrangement
# exists to catch -- so a checkout that normalised its line endings would break
# the one test whose job is failing on purpose.
site/e2e/fixtures/*.woff2 binary
